INSERT INTO `personalized_course_lessons` VALUES (7,2,1,'Section 1: Sentence Structure and Agreement','Subject-Verb Agreement: Singular vs. Plural','<p>Subject-verb agreement means that the subject and verb in a sentence must match in number: singular subjects take singular verbs, and plural subjects take plural verbs. This seems simple, but tricky cases often cause errors.</p><ul><li><strong>Compound subjects joined by \'and\'</strong> are usually plural: \'The manager and the client are happy.\'</li><li><strong>Subjects joined by \'or\' or \'nor\'</strong>: the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. Example: \'Neither the account manager nor the SDRs are available.\'</li><li><strong>Indefinite pronouns</strong> like \'each\', \'everyone\', \'nobody\' are singular: \'Each of the interpreters is required to submit a form.\'</li><li><strong>Collective nouns</strong> (team, committee) can be singular or plural depending on context, but in American English they are usually singular.</li><li><strong>Words like \'discrepancy\'</strong> are singular: \'The discrepancy between the two reports is significant.\'</li></ul><p>Always identify the simple subject and ignore intervening phrases. Practice by reading sentences aloud and listening for agreement.</p>','[\"Match singular subjects with singular verbs, plural with plural.\",\"With \'or\'\\/\'nor\', verb agrees with the nearest subject.\",\"Indefinite pronouns like \'each\' are singular.\",\"Ignore phrases between subject and verb.\",\"Collective nouns are usually singular in American English.\"]','Write 5 sentences using \'each of\', \'neither...nor\', and \'the team\' as subjects. Then check the verb agreement.','video','heygen','0fd48a2dca9f4cc99be8ee83ff20a2be',NULL,NULL,'Subject-Verb Agreement: Singular vs. Plural',NULL,'subject verb agreement rules examples beginner','pending','7659d123eb504146959c7794a61a1e8a','You\'ve probably heard that subjects and verbs must agree, but it can get tricky. Let\'s break it down. First, find your subject. Is it singular or plural? Then choose the verb that matches. Watch out for words like \'each\' or \'everyone\'—they are always singular. Also, when you have \'neither...nor\', look at the noun closest to the verb. For example, \'Neither the manager nor the assistants are here.\' Practice by writing your own sentences and saying them out loud. You\'ll get the hang of it!',NULL,'2026-05-21 06:17:21',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 05:35:53','2026-05-21 06:17:21'),(8,2,2,'Section 1: Sentence Structure and Agreement','Correct Word Choice: Subjunctive Mood and Common Confusions','<p>Choosing the correct word often depends on understanding grammatical mood and common word pairs. One frequent error is the subjunctive mood, used for wishes, demands, or suggestions. In the subjunctive, the verb form is always the base form (no -s). For example: \'The client requested that the interpreter be present.\' (not \'is\' or \'was\').</p><p>Other common confusions include:</p><ul><li><strong>Affect vs. Effect</strong>: \'Affect\' is usually a verb (to influence), \'effect\' is usually a noun (result).</li><li><strong>Its vs. It\'s</strong>: \'Its\' is possessive; \'it\'s\' is a contraction for \'it is\'.</li><li><strong>There/Their/They\'re</strong>: \'There\' is a place, \'their\' is possessive, \'they\'re\' is \'they are\'.</li><li><strong>Then vs. Than</strong>: \'Then\' indicates time; \'than\' is used for comparisons.</li></ul><p>Pay attention to context and practice with exercises.</p>','[\"Use the base verb after verbs like \'request\', \'suggest\', \'demand\' (subjunctive).\",\"Distinguish affect\\/effect, its\\/it\'s, there\\/their\\/they\'re, then\\/than.\",\"Context clues help choose the right word.\",\"Practice with fill-in-the-blank exercises.\"]','Complete these sentences: 1. The manager insisted that the report ___ (be) submitted. 2. ___ (Its/It\'s) important to check ___ (your/you\'re) work. 3. The effect was greater than we expected. Write 3 more similar sentences.','audio',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Sometimes you need to use a special verb form called the subjunctive. It\'s used after words like \'request\', \'suggest\', or \'insist\'. For example, \'I suggest that he study more.\' Notice we don\'t say \'studies\'. The verb stays in its base form. Also, watch out for common word pairs like \'affect\' and \'effect\'. A quick tip: \'affect\' is an action, \'effect\' is an end result. Practice by writing sentences with \'request that\' and \'insist that\'. You\'ll master it in no time!',NULL,NULL,'/storage/personalized_audio/2a9585d0-e40a-4485-bd3b-3dc3f11540e2.wav','ready','gemini_tts','2026-05-14 05:35:54','2026-05-19 09:48:48'),(9,2,3,'Section 2: Punctuation and Style','Using Semicolons Correctly','<p>The semicolon (;) connects two independent clauses that are closely related in meaning. It is stronger than a comma but weaker than a period. Use it when you want to show a relationship without a conjunction.</p><ul><li><strong>Between two independent clauses</strong>: \'The meeting ended; everyone left satisfied.\'</li><li><strong>With conjunctive adverbs</strong>: \'She was tired; however, she continued working.\' Note the semicolon before the adverb and a comma after.</li><li><strong>In complex lists</strong>: When list items already contain commas, use semicolons to separate them. Example: \'We visited Paris, France; Rome, Italy; and Berlin, Germany.\'</li></ul><p>Do not use a semicolon to introduce a list (use a colon instead). Also, avoid using a semicolon with a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or) – use a comma instead.</p>','[\"Semicolons connect two independent clauses without a conjunction.\",\"Use before conjunctive adverbs like \'however\', \'therefore\'.\",\"Use in complex lists to avoid confusion.\",\"Do not use with coordinating conjunctions.\",\"Do not use to introduce a list.\"]','Combine these pairs using a semicolon: 1. The sun set. The stars appeared. 2. He wanted to go. She preferred to stay. Then write a sentence using \'however\' with a semicolon.','text',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'how to use semicolons correctly grammar',NULL,NULL,'Semicolons might seem tricky, but they\'re just a way to connect two complete sentences that are closely related. Think of it as a soft period. For example, \'I love grammar; it makes writing clear.\' You can also use it with words like \'however\' or \'therefore\'. Just put a semicolon before and a comma after. Avoid using it with \'and\' or \'but\'—use a comma instead. Practice by taking two short sentences and joining them with a semicolon. You\'ll see how elegant your writing becomes!',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 05:35:56','2026-05-16 04:18:54'),(10,2,4,'Section 2: Punctuation and Style','Avoiding Dangling Modifiers and Passive Voice','<p>A dangling modifier is a word or phrase that modifies a word not clearly stated in the sentence. This leads to confusion or absurdity. Example: \'Walking to the store, the rain started.\' (Who was walking? The rain?) Fix it by adding the subject: \'Walking to the store, I felt the rain start.\'</p><p>Passive voice occurs when the subject receives the action instead of doing it. Example: \'The report was written by the intern.\' Active voice: \'The intern wrote the report.\' Active voice is usually more direct and concise.</p><ul><li><strong>Identify dangling modifiers</strong>: Look for introductory phrases that don\'t have a clear subject.</li><li><strong>Fix dangling modifiers</strong>: Rewrite the sentence so the subject follows the phrase.</li><li><strong>Prefer active voice</strong>: Make the doer the subject.</li><li><strong>When to use passive</strong>: When the doer is unknown or unimportant, or to emphasize the receiver.</li></ul>','[\"Dangling modifiers occur when the subject of the modifier is missing.\",\"Place the subject immediately after the introductory phrase.\",\"Active voice is clearer and more direct.\",\"Use passive voice sparingly and intentionally.\",\"Rewrite sentences to fix both issues.\"]','Rewrite these sentences: 1. \'Driving to work, a deer crossed the road.\' 2. \'The cake was eaten by the children.\' Then write two original sentences in active voice.','text',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'dangling modifiers passive voice grammar lesson',NULL,NULL,'Let\'s talk about two common writing problems: dangling modifiers and passive voice. A dangling modifier is a phrase that doesn\'t clearly connect to the subject. For example, \'Running late, the bus was missed.\' Who was running late? Fix it by saying \'Running late, I missed the bus.\' Passive voice happens when the subject is acted upon, like \'The ball was thrown by me.\' Active voice is stronger: \'I threw the ball.\' Try to use active voice most of the time. Practice by rewriting passive sentences to active. Your writing will become more lively and clear!',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 05:35:57','2026-05-16 04:18:54'),(11,2,5,'Section 2: Punctuation and Style','Parallel Structure in Sentences','<p>Parallel structure means using the same grammatical form for items in a list or series. This makes sentences easier to read and more balanced. For example: \'She likes swimming, running, and biking.\' (all gerunds) Not: \'She likes swimming, to run, and biking.\'</p><p>Common areas for parallel structure:</p><ul><li><strong>Lists</strong>: All items should be nouns, verbs, or phrases of the same type.</li><li><strong>Pairs with \'and\' or \'or\'</strong>: \'He is smart and hardworking.\' (both adjectives)</li><li><strong>Comparisons with \'than\' or \'as\'</strong>: \'She is more interested in reading than in writing.\' (both gerund phrases)</li><li><strong>Correlative conjunctions</strong>: \'Either you come with us or you stay here.\' (both clauses)</li></ul><p>Check for parallelism by reading the sentence aloud and ensuring each item flows naturally.</p>','[\"Use the same grammatical form for all items in a series.\",\"Match nouns with nouns, verbs with verbs, etc.\",\"Pay attention to pairs with \'and\', \'or\', \'than\', \'as\'.\",\"Correlative conjunctions require parallel clauses.\",\"Reading aloud helps spot errors.\"]','Fix the parallel structure: 1. \'She enjoys reading, to write, and painting.\' 2. \'He is not only smart but also has a good sense of humor.\' Write a correct version.','audio',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Parallel structure is all about balance. When you list items, make sure they are all the same type. For example, \'I like hiking, swimming, and biking\'—all gerunds. Avoid mixing forms like \'I like hiking, to swim, and biking.\' Also, watch for pairs with \'and\' or \'or\'. Say \'She is kind and generous\' not \'She is kind and has generosity.\' Practice by writing lists and checking each item. Your sentences will sound much smoother and more professional!',NULL,NULL,'/storage/personalized_audio/c2c2f0ec-b53d-429a-855a-ecb850bedd22.wav','ready','gemini_tts','2026-05-14 05:35:58','2026-05-19 09:48:48'),(12,3,1,'Punctuation Precision','Semicolons and Apostrophes: Getting Them Right','<p>Semicolons connect two closely related independent clauses without a conjunction. For example: <strong>She finished the report; he reviewed it.</strong> A common error is using a semicolon before a dependent clause or a list. Apostrophes show possession or contraction. For possession: <strong>the manager\'s report</strong> (singular) vs. <strong>the managers\' meeting</strong> (plural). Contractions: <strong>it\'s</strong> (it is) vs. <strong>its</strong> (possessive). Avoid using apostrophes for plurals.</p><ul><li>Use a semicolon between two complete sentences that are closely related.</li><li>Do not use a semicolon before a list; use a colon instead.</li><li>For singular possession, add \'s: <strong>the client\'s file</strong>.</li><li>For plural possession ending in s, add only an apostrophe: <strong>the clients\' files</strong>.</li><li>Remember: <strong>its</strong> is possessive; <strong>it\'s</strong> is a contraction.</li></ul>','[\"Semicolons join two independent clauses.\",\"Apostrophes indicate possession or contraction.\",\"Its vs. it\'s: possessive vs. contraction.\",\"Plural possessive: add apostrophe after s.\"]','Write two sentences using a semicolon correctly. Then write two sentences showing singular possession and two showing plural possession.','video','heygen','a3d314558be14d27a562dac93f7b6884','/storage/personalized_video/lesson-12-30267ee3-2614-4c82-bd46-858d8cbe68c3.mp4',NULL,'Semicolons and Apostrophes: Getting Them Right','https://files2.heygen.ai/aws_pacific/avatar_tmp/692ed713725f4d0fb87d05805a8e5602/fcdec1d7323c438794b1ab6304acf6d5.jpeg?Expires=1779913282&Signature=Kbo~dPfzV8g6AYGPt8GqbeCT5vZZVZVDa81w0YV8L2aUS6BKeUXAvO5Ev2wWn5rjgLjJl1tZR69f8PVhfIZ2qY0knoDZLjXwuWno3tiwLu-nNoJe4~WUSmLWOZjk4dY5L~2kx9t4Uvqc6UcbkRH0hZtqLvni7cpRgqv4OX0fsDY4dCJyeDR~E9461ZqYCq4EUyyJR5ZiY2BsyZ5qCxgL0ZBP23NxBINkRivIds~1OWDUQi0mpl3Ak9rqiqxBgU-Rn1tmLp6YPvA71OKHLfi63Zjsm1-UWXY5LGLljAH63d8zL-XXic8s-XIIG5bu3dJuOZksZQgWXV0adQMNm7z0Yw__&Key-Pair-Id=K38HBHX5LX3X2H','semicolon and apostrophe usage rules','ready','fcdec1d7323c438794b1ab6304acf6d5','You\'ve got this! Let\'s tackle semicolons and apostrophes. Semicolons are like a soft period—they connect two complete thoughts without a conjunction. For example: \'I love grammar; it\'s fun.\' Apostrophes show ownership or shorten words. \'The manager\'s pen\' means one manager. \'The managers\' pens\' means many managers. And remember: \'its\' is possessive, \'it\'s\' is short for \'it is\'. Practice by writing your own examples. You\'ll master these in no time!',NULL,'2026-05-21 06:17:23','/storage/personalized_audio/044f6d94-137d-4545-aa2a-8463f05d70f9.wav','ready','gemini_tts','2026-05-14 05:46:54','2026-05-21 06:21:32'),(13,3,2,'Punctuation Precision','Correcting Punctuation Errors in Context','<p>Many punctuation errors arise from confusing similar marks. For example, a comma splice occurs when a comma joins two independent clauses without a conjunction. Correct it with a period, semicolon, or conjunction. Another common error is missing commas after introductory phrases. Example: <strong>After the meeting, we reviewed the notes.</strong> Also, ensure that commas separate items in a list: <strong>apples, oranges, and bananas</strong>. Watch for run-on sentences and fragments.</p><ul><li>Fix comma splices by using a period or semicolon.</li><li>Use a comma after an introductory clause or phrase.</li><li>In lists, use the Oxford comma for clarity.</li><li>Avoid sentence fragments; ensure each sentence has a subject and verb.</li></ul>','[\"Comma splices: join with period or semicolon.\",\"Introductory phrases need a comma.\",\"Use Oxford comma in lists.\",\"Check for complete sentences.\"]','Find three sentences online or in a book that contain punctuation errors. Rewrite them correctly.','audio',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Punctuation errors are common but easy to fix. A comma splice happens when you join two sentences with just a comma. Instead, use a period or semicolon. For example: \'I went home, I was tired\' should be \'I went home; I was tired.\' Also, always put a comma after an introductory phrase like \'After the meeting\'. And don\'t forget the Oxford comma in lists: \'apples, oranges, and bananas\'. Practice by editing a paragraph. You\'ll become a punctuation pro!',NULL,NULL,'/storage/personalized_audio/e702ed26-4c5f-4fc4-95d8-f47d9c1a8f36.wav','ready','gemini_tts','2026-05-14 05:46:55','2026-05-19 09:41:51'),(14,3,3,'Subject-Verb Agreement and Word Choice','Mastering Subject-Verb Agreement','<p>Subject-verb agreement means the verb must match the subject in number. Singular subjects take singular verbs; plural subjects take plural verbs. Watch for tricky subjects: <strong>Neither the account manager nor the SDR</strong> – when using \'neither...nor\', the verb agrees with the closest subject. So: <strong>Neither the account manager nor the SDR is available</strong> (SDR is singular). Similarly, \'each\' is singular: <strong>Each of our interpreters is prepared</strong>. \'The data\' can be singular or plural; in business English, it\'s often singular: <strong>The data shows</strong>.</p><ul><li>Singular subjects need singular verbs (e.g., he runs).</li><li>With \'neither...nor\', verb agrees with the nearest subject.</li><li>\'Each\' is always singular.</li><li>\'Data\' is often treated as singular in formal writing.</li></ul>','[\"Subject and verb must agree in number.\",\"Neither\\/nor: verb agrees with closest subject.\",\"Each, every, either are singular.\",\"Data can be singular or plural; singular is common.\"]','Write five sentences using \'neither...nor\' with correct verb agreement. Then write five sentences using \'each\' as the subject.','text',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'subject verb agreement neither nor each',NULL,NULL,'Subject-verb agreement can be tricky, but you can master it. The key is to match the verb to the subject. For \'neither...nor\', look at the noun closest to the verb. \'Neither the manager nor the SDR is available\' – \'SDR\' is singular, so \'is\'. \'Each\' is always singular: \'Each of the interpreters is prepared\'. And remember, \'data\' is often singular in business: \'The data shows improvement\'. Practice by writing sentences with these tricky subjects. You\'ll get it right every time!',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 05:46:56','2026-05-16 04:18:55'),(15,3,4,'Subject-Verb Agreement and Word Choice','Choosing the Right Word: Common Confusions','<p>Word choice errors often involve homophones or similar words. \'Writing\' vs. \'writting\' – correct spelling is \'writing\' (one \'t\'). \'Maybe\' (perhaps) vs. \'may be\' (might be). Example: <strong>Maybe we should go</strong> vs. <strong>It may be raining</strong>. \'Inconvenience\' is often misspelled as \'inconveniance\' – correct is \'inconvenience\'. Also, \'affect\' vs. \'effect\': affect is a verb, effect is a noun. \'Their\', \'there\', \'they\'re\' are common confusions.</p><ul><li>\'Writing\' has one \'t\'.</li><li>\'Maybe\' is an adverb; \'may be\' is a verb phrase.</li><li>\'Inconvenience\' ends with -ence.</li><li>\'Affect\' (verb) vs. \'effect\' (noun).</li><li>\'Their\' (possessive), \'there\' (place), \'they\'re\' (they are).</li></ul>','[\"Spelling: writing (one t), inconvenience (-ence).\",\"Maybe vs. may be: adverb vs. verb phrase.\",\"Affect vs. effect: verb vs. noun.\",\"Their, there, they\'re: different meanings.\"]','Write a paragraph using \'maybe\', \'may be\', \'affect\', and \'effect\' correctly. Then write sentences using \'their\', \'there\', and \'they\'re\'.','text',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'commonly confused words affect effect maybe',NULL,NULL,'Choosing the right word is crucial for clear writing. \'Writing\' has one \'t\' – not \'writting\'. \'Maybe\' means perhaps, while \'may be\' means might be. For example: \'Maybe we can meet\' vs. \'It may be late\'. \'Inconvenience\' is spelled with -ence. And \'affect\' is a verb (to influence), \'effect\' is a noun (result). Also, remember \'their\' shows possession, \'there\' is a place, and \'they\'re\' means they are. Practice by writing sentences with each. You\'ll avoid these common mistakes!',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 05:46:58','2026-05-16 04:18:55');

